    {
      "ordinal": 3,
      "depth": 0,
      "kind": "section",
      "number": "2",
      "title": "Definition",
      "body": "Nasal and Breteche bascinets are medieval helmets that originated in the early 14th century. They are an evolution of simple bascinets worn with chainmail. The chainmail aventail is attached to the front of the helmet to increase its face protection.\n\nNasal bascinet uses a solid metal nasal to protect the nose, attached on the forehead by a piece of metal, the “spoon”.\n\nBreteche bascinet uses chainmail to protect the nose, attached on the forehead by a piece of metal, the “cross”.\n\nBoth helmets were used from 1340 to the late 14th century in Western Europe.\nNasal and breteche bascinets are Approved for 14th century and Transitional armors.\n\nNasal and breteche bascinets are Denied for 15th century armors.\n\nSpoon Cross",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
      "stable_key": "nasals-and-breteches:2",
      "parent_key": null,
